  <section id="human-outbound-calling" class="home-calling-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">Human-led outbound calling</div>
      <h2>Calling is not a button. It is a live sales conversation.</h2>
      <p>CallTeam callers contact decision-makers on behalf of the campaign, explain the reason for the call, listen to the response, work through the first resistance, ask the right questions, and decide whether a next step makes sense. That is different from simply reading a script and counting dials.</p>
      <p>Some calls are cold. Others follow an inbound form, a previous conversation, an event, a referral, or an old opportunity. The opening and the goal should match the situation. A person who downloaded something yesterday should not receive the same call as a decision-maker who has never heard of the company.</p>
      <p>Calls also reveal what lists and dashboards cannot: whether the role is correct, whether the problem matters, how buyers describe it, which objections appear repeatedly, who else should be involved, and whether the market understands the offer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="talk-tracks" class="home-talk-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Talk tracks and message approval</div>
      <h2>Your company should know how it will be represented.</h2>
      <div class="home-talk-grid">
        <article><span>1</span><h3>Campaign brief</h3><p>We learn the offer, buyer, problem, proof, common objections, boundaries, and the reason a prospect should care.</p></article>
        <article><span>2</span><h3>Approved language</h3><p>We align on the opening, core message, qualification questions, meeting standard, and claims that should or should not be used.</p></article>
        <article><span>3</span><h3>Real-call learning</h3><p>We use actual responses to make the language clearer. Changes should come from evidence, not random script rewrites.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="home-centered-copy">A talk track gives the caller structure. It should not trap the caller inside a robotic speech. Good callers know the destination of the conversation and still listen to the person in front of them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="follow-up" class="home-followup-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">Structured follow-up</div>
      <h2>One unanswered call is not a sales strategy.</h2>
      <p>Most good prospects are busy. They miss calls, ask for information, send the caller to another person, delay a decision, or say “not now” when they really mean “the timing is unclear.” A campaign needs a follow-up rhythm that keeps the opportunity moving without turning into noise.</p>
      <p>CallTeam can manage agreed call attempts and appropriate supporting follow-up around the campaign. The exact channels, timing, number of touches, message, and stop rules depend on the engagement. Replies, referrals, clear declines, wrong-person outcomes, and booked conversations should all change what happens next.</p>
      <div class="home-followup-rail"><span>Call</span><b>→</b><span>Record the outcome</span><b>→</b><span>Take the right next action</span><b>→</b><span>Stop when the answer is clear</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="lead-qualification" class="home-qualification-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Lead qualification</div>
      <h2>A full calendar means nothing if the wrong people are on it.</h2>
      <p class="home-section-intro">Before launch, we agree on what must be true for a meeting to count. The exact questions change by offer, but the qualification logic is simple.</p>
      <div class="home-qualification-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Company fit</h3><p>Does the company match the market, size, location, industry, or operating profile the offer is built for?</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Role relevance</h3><p>Is this the right person, a useful influencer, a clear referral, or someone connected to the decision?</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Need or reason</h3><p>Is there a real problem, goal, change, gap, or business reason that makes a conversation useful?</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Interest and timing</h3><p>Is there enough interest to continue, and does the timing support a real next step now or later?</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Decision path</h3><p>Who else matters, what happens next, and what must the sales team understand before the meeting?</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Clear next step</h3><p>Did the prospect knowingly agree to a relevant conversation, with a reason for both sides to attend?</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="appointment-setting" class="home-handoff-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">Appointment setting and handoff</div>
      <h2>Your sales team should know why the meeting exists.</h2>
      <p>A booked meeting is the handoff point, not the finish line. The calendar invitation should connect the right people to a clear next conversation. The notes should help your seller walk in prepared instead of asking the prospect to repeat everything.</p>
      <p>Depending on the campaign and agreed process, the handoff can include the contact and company, the reason the prospect agreed to talk, useful needs or priorities, timing, current approach, objections, other people involved, relevant call notes, and the agreed next step. If the prospect is not ready for a meeting but should be followed later, that outcome should be recorded instead of forced onto the calendar.</p>
      <div class="home-handoff-card"><span>Qualified handoff</span><strong>Right account · Right person · Real reason · Clear next step · Useful context</strong></div>
    </div>
  </section>

